From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:47:18 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 193/194] hjviewer: the phone app asks the PC for a new picture
 once, a few seconds after the first
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Right after connecting from the iPhone to a Windows PC with two monitors the picture
sometimes stays faint and looks like a low, broken resolution until the user switches
monitors and back. The menu's 새로 고침 restarts the PC's video service as a switch does (a
new capturer and encoder, a new full picture at the session's ratio of that moment), though
unlike a switch it keeps the phone's decoder; the mobile remote page now asks for it once by
itself.

The delay is 6 s after the connection's first picture. The PC's video QoS
(src/server/video_qos.rs) holds each new viewer at INIT_FPS (15) for its first second,
probes the delay once a second and doubles the fps after two clean replies; the bitrate
is cut after two bad replies in a row (deeper after three), at most once per 3 s, the fps
after three. 6 s after the first picture, which itself comes about a second after the
connection opens, the fps start-up and a congestion episode from the start-up burst are
over on a good link. A cut made at start-up stays on a still screen (the ratio rises only
on a moving one), so the refresh then encodes at that lowered ratio; the refresh itself
does not reset the QoS state, as the viewer stays.
Later than that only keeps the bad picture up longer.

HjvAutoRefresh (plain Dart, tested) arms on each first picture, keeps at most one timer
and refreshes only if, at its time, the page is mounted, the session not closed, the
connection has its picture (no reconnect waiting for a new one) and no refresh or user
display switch was asked for since the first picture. PeerInfo counts those requests in
sessionRefreshVideo and in openMonitorInTheSameTab when the user chose the display (not
the automatic restore after a reconnect, which needs the refresh as much). The first-image
callbacks stay registered across a reconnect, whose new peer info sets waitForFirstImage
again, so a reconnect brings one more first picture: it re-arms the same timer (one per
connection, never two). dispose cancels it.

A refresh does not move the phone's zoom, pan or display: the PC sends the display info
only when the display really changed (check_display_changed), the client's
handleSwitchDisplay updates the view only when the display's rect changed, and the
image model keeps its image, so the canvas is not initialised again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
